The docs now say plainly that -p, --print, a bare yoyo "…" and piped yoyo approve every tool call without asking, with or without -y. No code or default changed: whether that should be the default is the owner's decision on #1149, so the commit is titled "Part of #1149".

What the code does. I read the two lines the assessment cited, and its claims hold:
• src/main.rs:1395–1396: a run counts as interactive only if stdin is a terminal and there's no prompt on the command line. Every other run is auto-approved.
• src/cli.rs:1374–1375: --print implies --yes.
• A bare positional prompt counts as a command-line prompt too (via cli_config::prompt_text), so it's auto-approved as well.
• In those runs bash gets no confirm step at all, so an --allow list narrows nothing. What still stops a command is the deny list and the built-in refusals (rm -rf / and /*, mkfs, dd onto a device, the fork bomb). The file tools keep their --allow-dir/--deny-dir check.

What changed in the docs.
• `permissions.md`:
  • The --yes example is now yoyo -y for the REPL. A new paragraph says non-interactive runs already approve everything, lists what still applies, and says to use a deny list or a sandbox on a repo you don't trust.
  • The opening "By default, yoyo prompts you" now says this is in the interactive REPL.
  • The "Sandboxed CI" example drops the -y it didn't need.
• `single-prompt.md` and `piped-mode.md`: each has a new "Tool approval" section with the same facts, linking to permissions.md. No issue numbers are in the user-facing text.

Left alone. The grep also found docs/src/usage/commands.md:8 (yoyo --yes -p doctor). The --yes there is unnecessary but not misleading, and it's a fourth file, so I listed it in the commit body without editing it. The grep's other hit in that file, line 546, matched on a long line by accident.

